  "theorem": {
    "common_period_moduli_criterion": "Every genuine finite-sided oriented H10 Coxeter polyhedron admitting an orientation-compatible realization by primitive negative vectors whose reflections preserve S=U+E8(-2)+<-4> is a weak period-level full-Picard divisor carrier, after choosing an S-marking of a very general K3 surface on the nine-dimensional S-polarized period locus.",
    "compact_no_go_inside_S": "No compact H10 Coxeter polytope admits a primitive-negative integral-mirror realization in S.",
    "converse_for_this_marked_period_locus": "Every primitive negative Picard vector defining an integral hyperbolic mirror reflection has type (-2,div1), (-4,div2), or (-4,div4). Self-pairing divisibility excludes (-2,div4), and the full discriminant form excludes (-2,div2) and (-8,div4).",
    "finite_volume_subgroup_criterion": "Let W(S) be generated by every primitive negative integral mirror reflection of S. Its complete 13-wall chamber has finite volume and three cusps. If a genuine Coxeter polyhedron P is the fundamental domain of its facet group Gamma<=W(S), then vol(P) is finite exactly when [W(S):Gamma] is finite, and every finite-volume such P is noncompact."
  }
